ase_nonce (string). Output must match the schema: decision (string), memory_append (null or object with reflection and signature), next_attempt (integer).\n\nThe objective: bounded trial loop. With no prior attempt, request attempt one. Stop immediately after success. Stop on the registered attempt budget or a repeated failure signature. Otherwise request exactly the next attempt. Append a nonempty reflection with its signature and return decision, next index, and memory update.\n\nInterpretation: We have attempts list already performed. We need to determine the next action. The \"attempts\" are previous attempts. The function should decide what to do next. The fields: success boolean, failure_signature string, reflection string. The reflection is presumably the reflection from that attempt. The \"reflection_after_feedback\" suggests that after each attempt, we have a reflection.\n\nWe need to decide: \n- If there is a successful attempt in the list, stop: decision = \"stop\", next_attempt = some index? But the schema says next_attempt is integer. Possibly the next attempt number to be performed (1-indexed). The \"next_attempt\" should be the next attempt number (starting from 1) or the index? The description: \"With no prior attempt request attempt one.\" So initial attempt number is 1. So attempt numbers are 1-based. If we have previous attempts, the current attempt number is len(attempts)+1? But we need to decide based on conditions.\n\nStop conditions:\n1. Any attempt with success=True -> stop. decision \"stop\". What about next_attempt? Possibly the current attempt number? Or maybe next_attempt is the attempt index we are at? But we are not requesting a next attempt if we stop. The schema requires next_attempt integer. Possibly we set next_attempt to the number of attempts? Let's examine the output schema: next_attempt is type integer. The description: \"otherwise request exactly the next attempt\". So when we are requesting a next attempt, we set next_attempt to the next attempt number (1-indexed). When we stop, maybe next_attempt is not meaningful but must be present. Could be set to the current attempt number? Or maybe we set it to 0? Need to infer from hints.\n\n2. Stop on registered attempt budget: meaning if we have reached max_attempts (i.e., number of attempts already performed == max_attempts?), then stop. Or if the next attempt would exceed budget? The attempt budget: \"Stop on the registered attempt budget\". Usually means if the number of attempts already performed equals max_attempts, then no more attempts allowed. But the attempts list includes past attempts. The budget is max_attempts. So if len(attempts) >= max_attempts, we should stop. Note: It says \"Stop on the registered attempt budget\". So if we have already used up the budget (i.e., number of attempts performed equals max_attempts), then stop. Not after performing the next one. So if len(attempts) >= max_attempts, stop.\n\n3. Stop on a repeated failure signature. That means if the failure_signature of the latest attempt matches any previous failure_signature? The description: \"a repeated failure signature.\" Likely means if the failure_signature of the last attempt appears earlier in the attempts list (excluding the last? Could be any repetition). We need to check for any duplicate failure_signature across all attempts? Or only consecutive? The phrase \"repeated failure signature\" could mean the same failure signature appears more than once. Typically in reflexion, you stop when you see the same failure signature again because you are stuck in a loop. So if there is any duplicate failure_signature among all attempts, stop? But careful: The first occurrence is fine, second occurrence (repetition) triggers stop. So if the set of failure_signatures has duplicates, then stop. But we need to consider only the most recent? Usually you detect when you encounter a failure signature you've seen before. So after appending the latest attempt, if its failure_signature matches any previous attempt's failure_signature, stop.\n\n4. Otherwise, request next attempt: decision = \"continue\", next_attempt = len(attempts) + 1 (since attempt numbers are 1-indexed). Also need to append a memory_append with the reflection from the last attempt (if any) because \"Append a nonempty reflection with its signature\". Wait: The output includes memory_append which can be null or an object with reflection and signature. The description: \"Append a nonempty reflection with its signature and return decision, next index, and memory update.\" So for each call, we are to return the memory update for that last attempt? The context: The state machine runs after each attempt? The case payload includes a list of attempts already done. The function should decide what to do next based on the state after those attempts. It should also output the memory_append that corresponds to the last attempt's reflection (if the last attempt had a reflection). But wait: The memory append is to be used by the system to store in memory. Apparently the function is invoked after each attempt? The description: \"trial_reflect_retry_controller\" through \"solve(case)\" for this bounded objective: Drive a bounded trial loop... with no prior attempt request attempt one. So the function is called with a case containing the history of attempts so far, and it returns the next action. The memory_append is what should be appended to memory after this decision? Or it's the reflection from the last attempt? The text: \"Append a nonempty reflection with its signature and return decision, next index, and memory update.\" I think the memory_append should contain the reflection and signature of the last attempt (the most recent attempt in the attempts list), because that is the reflection that was generated. The \"nonempty\" means we should only append if there is a reflection (i.e., the last attempt has a reflection string that is nonempty). In the fixtures, each attempt has a \"reflection\" field. So we should output that reflection and its failure_signature (the signature of the failure? Or the reflection's own signature? The schema says signature is a string. In the attempts, there's failure_signature. So likely the signature for the memory_append should be the failure_signature of that attempt. The reflection is the reflection string from that attempt.\n\nBut what about when it's the first call with no prior attempts? Then attempts list is empty. The description: \"With no prior attempt request attempt one.\" So decision = \"continue\", next_attempt = 1. memory_append should be null because there is no reflection to append. So memory_append null.\n\nWhen we stop due to success, we still need to append the memory for the successful attempt? The description: \"Stop immediately after success.\" So the last attempt was successful. We need to append its reflection? Probably yes, because we still want to record that reflection. So memory_append should contain the reflection and signature from the successful attempt (the last one). Decision \"stop\". next_attempt? Possibly set to the current attempt number? Or maybe to 0? The schema says integer. In the stop case, next_attempt may be ignored, but must be present. Could set to len(attempts) (the last attempt number). Or maybe set to -1? I think it's safer to set to the number of attempts performed (which is the last attempt number). The description: \"return decision, next index, and memory update.\" Next index likely refers to the next attempt number. When stopping, there is no next attempt, so maybe it's not used but must be present. I'll set to 0 or the last attempt number? The fixtures might expect a specific value. Let's examine the given fixtures: They show three cases. For case a-000: attempts list has 3 attempts, third is success, max_attempts=4. So after these attempts, the function should decide to stop because success. The expected output would be: decision \"stop\", next_attempt? Possibly 4? Or 3? The next attempt if continued would be 4, but we stop. Possibly they want next_attempt = 4 (the next trial number that would have been attempted but is not), or maybe the index of the last attempt? Not clear. Since the output contract says next_attempt is integer, and the description \"return decision, next index, and memory update\" suggests next_attempt is the index of the next attempt to make (1-indexed). So when we stop, we could set it to something like -1 or 0 to indicate no next. But schema doesn't forbid 0 or negative. However, typical designs: set to 0 or null? But schema says integer, not null. I think it's more likely that next_attempt is the next attempt number (1-indexed) if continue, and if stop, it could be the current attempt number (the last one). The problem statement: \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, implementation. Its value must be Python source defining solve(case). The function must return a JSON-serializable object.\" So we have freedom to choose. But we need to be consistent with expected behavior.\n\nLet's reason from the fixture input. The case a-000 has 3 attempts, max_attempts=4. If we call solve(case) with that payload, what would the expected output be? We have a success at attempt 3. So we stop. The memory_append should contain the reflection from the last attempt (attempt 3: reflection \"adjust-2\", failure_signature \"e2\" but success true). So memory_append = {\"reflection\": \"adjust-2\", \"signature\": \"e2\"}? But note: The attempt's success is true, so failure_signature is \"e2\". That seems weird: a successful attempt still has a failure_signature? Possibly it's the signature of the failure that occurred before the reflection? Or maybe it's just a label. The fixtures show all attempts have failure_signature, even successful ones. So we use that.\n\nNow next_attempt: Since we stop, what value? Could be 3 (the last attempt number) or 0. I'll inspect the third case a-002: attempts list has 3 attempts,